urname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Doherty</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Ornstein</surname><given-names>PL</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>The potent mGlu receptor antagonist LY341495 identifies roles for both cloned and novel mGlu receptors in hippocampal synaptic plasticity.</article-title><source>Neuropharmacology</source><volume>37</volume><fpage>1445</fpage><lpage>1458</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9886667</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006122-Mannaioni1"><label>14</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mannaioni</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Marino</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Valenti</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Traynelis</surname><given-names>SF</given-names></name><name><surname>Conn</surname><given-names>PJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Metabotropic glutamate receptors 1 and 5 differentially regulate CA1 pyramidal cell function.</article-title><source>J Neurosci</source><volume>21</volume><fpage>5925</fpage><lpage>5934</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11487615</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006122-Volk1"><label>15</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Volk</surname><given-names>LJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Daly</surname><given-names>CA</given-names></name><name><surname>Huber</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Differential roles for group 1 mGluR subtypes in induction and expression of chemically induced hippocampal long-term depression.</article-title><source>J Neurophysiol</source><volume>95</volume><fpage>2427</fpage><lpage>2438</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16421200</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006122-Watabe1"><label>16</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Watabe</surname><given-names>AM</given-names></name><name><surname>Carlisle</surname><given-names>HJ</given-names></name><name><surname>O'Dell</surname><given-names>TJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Postsynaptic induction and presynaptic expression of group 1 mGluR-dependent LTD in the hippocampal CA1 region.</article-title><source>J Neurophysiol</source><volume>87</volume><fpage>1395</fpage><lpage>1403</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11877514</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006122-Gasparini1"><label>17</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gasparini</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Lingenhohl</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Stoehr</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Flor</surname><given-names>PJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Heinrich</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>2-Methyl-6-(phenylethynyl)-pyridine (MPEP), a potent, selective and systemically active mGlu5 receptor antagonist.</article-title><source>Neuropharmacology</source><volume>38</volume><fpage>1493</fpage><lpage>1503</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10530811</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006122-Knopfel1"><label>18</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Knopfel</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Two new non-competitive mGlu1 receptor antagonists are potent tools to unravel functions of this mGlu receptor subtype.</article-title><source>Br J Pharmacol</source><volume>151</volume><fpage>723</fpage><lpage>724</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17502846</pub-id></mixed-ci